  "slug": "association-adjusting-2e22",
  "summary": "Recent methods address collider bias, medication confounding, LD reference mismatch, and replicability within association workflows.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is correction of inference bias introduced by covariates, confounders, and reference mismatch in genomic association workflows. A Gaussian liability-model conversion from log odds corrects collider bias in covariate-adjusted binary GWAS, and GEMRA adjusts phenotypes for medication use, reporting less bias and higher power than common approaches in simulations. RSparsePro addresses linkage disequilibrium reference mismatch in fine-mapping using a probabilistic graphical model with variational inference. KDRD estimates replicability of significant associations from one study's summary statistics, tested on UK Biobank. MRGN extends causal network inference to genomic trios with high-dimensional confounders. The new elements are phenotype-level and summary-statistic-level corrections applied within association workflows, alongside a causal-inference method for confounded trio data.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-08"
}
